\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {16}{\ignorespaces Coverage of nominal 95 percent intervals across 2,000 simulated populations per cell. Bias is the mean estimate minus the population $\Lambda $. Noise gives the item-noise standard deviation relative to unit run effects. Inversion is the centred cluster test-inversion interval, computed later on the same simulated moments. Every cell was afterwards repeated at 10,000 replicates, and the main text quotes those ranges rather than the columns here, so the two differ by a few thousandths.\relax }}{37}{table.caption.37}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:coverage}{{16}{37}{Coverage of nominal 95 percent intervals across 2,000 simulated populations per cell. Bias is the mean estimate minus the population $\Lambda $. Noise gives the item-noise standard deviation relative to unit run effects. Inversion is the centred cluster test-inversion interval, computed later on the same simulated moments. Every cell was afterwards repeated at 10,000 replicates, and the main text quotes those ranges rather than the columns here, so the two differ by a few thousandths.\relax }{table.caption.37}{}}
